    <%# Development Server %>
    <section id="development-server">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Development Server" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>The development server runs as two systemd user services that start automatically at boot.</p>

        <table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 border border-gray-200 rounded-md overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-gray-50">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Service</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Purpose</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="bg-white divide-y divide-gray-100 text-xs">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">boolder-rails</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Puma web server on port 3000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">boolder-css</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Tailwind CSS watcher — recompiles on file changes</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Managing the services</p>
        <pre class="bg-gray-800 text-gray-100 text-xs rounded-md p-4 overflow-x-auto"># Status
systemctl --user status boolder-rails boolder-css

# Stop / start / restart
systemctl --user stop boolder-rails boolder-css
systemctl --user start boolder-rails boolder-css
systemctl --user restart boolder-rails

# Follow logs
journalctl --user -u boolder-rails -f
journalctl --user -u boolder-css -f</pre>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Service files</p>
        <p>Located at <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">~/.config/systemd/user/</code>. Environment variables are loaded from the project's <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">.env</code> file. Boot auto-start requires <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">loginctl enable-linger ed</code> (already configured).</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Backups %>
    <section id="backups">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Backups" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Daily automated backups run via a systemd timer and rsync to hosted webspace at <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">edsouthwood.com</code>.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">What is backed up</p>
        <table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 border border-gray-200 rounded-md overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-gray-50">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Item</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Webspace location</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="bg-white divide-y divide-gray-100 text-xs">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">PostgreSQL database</td><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">bowda-backup/db/</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Compressed pg_dump, timestamped, 7-day rolling retention</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">Uploaded files</td><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">bowda-backup/storage/</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Incremental rsync — current state only</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">config/master.key</td><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">bowda-backup/master.key</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Required to decrypt credentials</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">.env</td><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">bowda-backup/.env</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Mapbox key, admin credentials</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Managing backups</p>
        <pre class="bg-gray-800 text-gray-100 text-xs rounded-md p-4 overflow-x-auto"># Run a backup immediately
bin/backup

# Check backup logs
tail -f log/backup.log

# Check timer status
systemctl --user status boolder-backup.timer</pre>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Recovery</p>
        <p>To restore after a machine failure:</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li>Clone the repo from GitHub</li>
          <li>Restore <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">.env</code> and <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">config/master.key</code> from the webspace</li>
          <li>Restore the database: <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">gunzip -c dartmoor-dev-YYYYMMDD.sql.gz | psql dartmoor-dev</code></li>
          <li>Restore storage files: <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">rsync -az u79222@edsouthwood.com:bowda-backup/storage/ storage/</code></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Error Monitoring %>
    <section id="error-monitoring">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Uptime & Error Monitoring" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Uptime monitoring</p>
        <p>Every 15 minutes a GitHub Actions workflow (<code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">.github/workflows/uptime.yml</code>) checks the live site from GitHub's servers: <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">/up</code> returns 200, the home page loads with its "Bouldering on Dartmoor" heading, and the HTTPS certificate has at least 14 days left. Each check retries for about two minutes first, so a brief blip doesn't alert. When a run fails, GitHub emails the account that last changed the workflow's schedule, so you get an email every 15 minutes until the site is back. Runs and their results are on the repository's <strong>Actions → Uptime</strong> page, where <strong>Run workflow</strong> checks on demand. The same checks run from the server with <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">UPTIME_RESOLVE=127.0.0.1 script/uptime_check.sh</code>.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Error monitoring</p>
        <p>Production errors are reported to <a href="https://app.bugsnag.com" class="text-granite-dark underline">Bugsnag</a>, tagged with the deployed git commit.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
          <li><strong>Browser</strong> (JavaScript errors only; nothing is sent unless an error happens): <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">app/views/layouts/_bugsnag.html.erb</code>, in every layout. Its API key is public by design. Offline pages skip it.</li>
          <li><strong>Server</strong> (Rails exceptions and background jobs): <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">config/initializers/bugsnag.rb</code>, using <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">bugsnag: api_key</code> from the production credentials. Without it, startup logs "No valid API key has been set".</li>
          <li>Visitor IP addresses are kept out of both, and nothing is stored on visitors' devices. The privacy policy describes this — update it if the setup changes.</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Content-Security-Policy</p>
        <p>Pages send a Content-Security-Policy in <strong>report-only</strong> mode: nothing is blocked, but anything that would be is logged as a <code>[CSP]</code> line in the production log (<code>journalctl --user -u boolder-rails | grep "\[CSP\]"</code>). Once real traffic reports nothing unexpected it can be enforced. Page code needs nonce'd inline scripts and no inline <code>onclick=</code>-style handlers; see the admin guide.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Admin Accounts %>
    <section id="admin-accounts">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Admin Accounts" %>
      <div class="prose-content space-y-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
        <p>Managed on the website under <strong>Accounts</strong> (super admins). Passwords are stored as bcrypt hashes.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
          <li><strong>Add an admin</strong>: username, role, and areas for an area admin. Leave the password blank for a strong one, shown once: send it privately</li>
          <li><strong>Edit</strong>: role, areas, new or random password, or <strong>Disabled</strong>. A new password or disabling signs them out everywhere at once</li>
          <li>Only disabled accounts can be deleted. Every change needs your own password and goes in the audit log. The last super admin can't be demoted or disabled</li>
          <li><strong>My account</strong> (your username, top right): change your own password (12+ characters)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Until an enabled super admin exists in Accounts, and as an emergency way back in, logins come from the old <code>admin_accounts</code> credentials list. On the server: <code>bin/rails admin:import_accounts</code> copies those across; <code>bin/rails admin:set_password ADMIN=name</code> sets (or creates) a super admin's password if you're locked out.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Login lockout</p>
        <p>After 10 wrong passwords from one IP address, admin login (including <code>/jobs</code>) is refused with "429 Too Many Requests" for 15 minutes from the first failure. To unlock early, in a Rails console on the server:</p>
        <pre class="bg-gray-800 text-gray-100 text-xs rounded-md p-4">Rails.cache.delete("admin_login_failures/&lt;ip address&gt;")</pre>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Signing in and out</p>
        <p>An admin login lasts 14 days, then the password is asked for again. <strong>Sign out</strong> (top right of the admin header) ends it at once; because browsers re-send a saved password by themselves, the next admin page deliberately asks for it once more.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Permission Levels %>
    <section id="permission-levels">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Permission Levels" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 border border-gray-200 rounded-md overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-gray-50">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Role</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Access</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="bg-white divide-y divide-gray-100">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-purple-700">super_admin</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2">Full access — create/delete areas, all imports, bulk uploads, POIs, audits</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-blue-700">area_admin</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2">Edit assigned areas and their problems, topos, lines; download/upload GeoJSON for assigned areas; view contributions</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li>Area creation and deletion — super admin only</li>
          <li>Circuits — super admin only</li>
          <li>Bulk uploads, POIs, audit log — super admin only</li>
          <li>GeoJSON import apply step — scoped to your assigned areas</li>
          <li>Problems can only be moved between areas you have access to</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Managing Areas %>
    <section id="managing-areas">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Managing Areas" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Areas are the top-level groupings. Each has problems, boulders, circuits, and POI routes.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">The area tool bar</p>
        <p>Every area admin page has tabs under the breadcrumbs: <strong>Problems</strong>, <strong>Boulders</strong>, <strong>Photos</strong> (topos and workbench), <strong>Map</strong> (location editor) and <strong>Outlines</strong> (boulder editor), with counts of what's waiting (boulders needing photos or lines, photos to draw, problems to place). <strong>+ Add</strong> has Upload photos, Import problem list and New problem; <strong>⋮</strong> has Edit area, Edit circuit, Map download and Map import. The Areas list has the same shortcuts in its Tools column.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Creating an area <span class="text-xs font-normal text-gray-400">(super admin only)</span></p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Go to <strong>Admin → Areas → New area</strong></li>
          <li>Fill in <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">name</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">slug</code> (URL-friendly, e.g. <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">bonehill-rocks</code>), descriptions</li>
          <li>Leave <strong>published</strong> unchecked until the area is ready. Until then its pages, problems and photos return "not found" to the public (even by guessing the address), while signed-in admins can still see them</li>
          <li>Add problems and boulders before publishing</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Tags</p>
        <p>Available: <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">popular</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">beginner_friendly</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">family_friendly</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">dry_fast</code></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Bulk Upload %>
    <section id="bulk-upload">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Bulk Upload (Problems)" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>The fastest way to add many problems to an area. Go to <strong>Admin → Bulk Uploads</strong>.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">CSV format</p>
        <pre class="bg-gray-800 text-gray-100 text-xs rounded-md p-4 overflow-x-auto">name,grade,steepness,sit_start,lat,lon,image_filename,ukc_url,description
Overhanging Scoop,5c,overhang,false,50.5712,-3.9341,scoop.jpg,,Classic line up the right side.
Low Traverse,4a,wall,false,50.5715,-3.9345,,,
Unnamed Slab,,slab,false,,,,</pre>

        <table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 border border-gray-200 rounded-md overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-gray-50">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Column</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Required</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="bg-white divide-y divide-gray-100 text-xs">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">name</td><td class="px-4 py-2">No</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Can be blank for unnamed problems</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">grade</td><td class="px-4 py-2">No</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Font grade: 4, 5, 5+, 6a, 6b+, 7a, etc.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">steepness</td><td class="px-4 py-2 font-semibold text-red-600">Yes</td><td class="px-4 py-2">See valid values below</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">sit_start</td><td class="px-4 py-2">No</td><td class="px-4 py-2"><code>true</code> or leave blank</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">lat / lon</td><td class="px-4 py-2">No</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Decimal degrees, e.g. 50.5712 / -3.9341</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">image_filename</td><td class="px-4 py-2">No</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Must match uploaded image filename exactly</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">ukc_url</td><td class="px-4 py-2">No</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Full UKC URL for the problem</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">description</td><td class="px-4 py-2">No</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Free-text description shown on the problem page</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Valid steepness values</p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs bg-gray-100 p-2 rounded"><%= Problem::STEEPNESS_VALUES.join(", ") %></p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Uploading images</p>
        <p>Upload image files alongside the CSV. Each image creates a topo — draw line coordinates on it afterwards in the topo editor.</p>

        <%= link_to "Go to Bulk Upload →", new_admin_bulk_upload_path, class: "inline-block mt-2 text-blue-600 hover:underline text-sm" %>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Boulder names and problem lists %>
    <section id="boulder-names">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Boulder Names & Problem Lists" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Boulders can have names, and each problem can say which boulder it's on before it has a location or photo.</p>
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Import problem list</p>
        <p>Area tool bar <strong>+ Add → Import problem list</strong>. Paste (or upload a <code>.txt</code>) each boulder's name on its own line, followed by its problems, one per line, starting with a tab: name, tab, grade (<code>f6B</code>, <code>6b</code>, <code>f4+</code> and <code>5</code> all work). An optional list of the names at the top makes any other untabbed lines count as notes, which are ignored. Only names and grades are read.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
          <li><strong>Preview</strong> first, then <strong>Import</strong>. Nothing is saved before that</li>
          <li><strong>matches</strong>: an existing problem with the same name (accents, case and apostrophes ignored) goes onto that boulder. Grades only change if you tick <strong>Change grade</strong></li>
          <li><strong>new</strong> problems are created (untick to leave one out); <strong>check</strong> rows have a near-identical name, so pick the existing problem, create a new one, or skip</li>
          <li>New boulders are created without an outline, ready to draw. Importing the same list again changes nothing</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Hiding problems</p>
        <p><strong>Hide</strong> keeps a problem but leaves it off the public site (its page is a 404 to the public) and out of the Boulders list counts and the workbench. Tick <strong>hide</strong> on an import preview row, or click <strong>Hide</strong> next to a problem on the Boulders list. Hidden ones are listed struck through with <strong>Show again</strong>; a hidden problem's admin page has a <strong>Show it again</strong> button.</p>
        <p><strong>Hide a whole boulder</strong> (e.g. out of scope for now): select it in the boulder editor and click <strong>Hide boulder from the public site</strong>, or expand it on the Boulders list and click <strong>Hide boulder</strong>. The outline and all its problems leave the public site and the Boulders list counts, and Mapping progress stops counting them. Nothing is deleted: hidden boulders are drawn faint amber in the editor and listed under <strong>Hidden boulders</strong> with <strong>Show again</strong>, which puts everything back as it was.</p>
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Boulders list</p>
        <p>Area tool bar → <strong>Boulders</strong> tab: every boulder, most urgent first. <strong>Not on map</strong> (draw it), <strong>Needs photos</strong> (upload), <strong>Needs lines</strong> (opens the workbench on its next photo), then <strong>Done</strong>. "Needs topos" hides finished boulders and outlines without problems. Click a name to see its problems (✓ = has a line); problems on no boulder are listed at the bottom.</p>
        <p>A located problem sitting on an outline joins that boulder automatically, but a boulder set by a list is never overwritten. A problem's <strong>Boulder</strong> menu on its edit form changes it by hand.</p>
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Naming outlines (boulder editor)</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Select an outline and click one of the listed names shown under <strong>Name</strong> (type to narrow the list), or type a new name and press Enter. Picking a listed name that isn't drawn yet gives the outline that boulder's problems</li>
          <li>Picked the wrong listed name? Choosing a second one asks first: <strong>OK</strong> swaps (the old name goes back on the list with its problems); <strong>Cancel</strong> then offers to merge both lots of problems instead. Workbench photo ties follow the name</li>
          <li><strong>Not on the map yet</strong> in the sidebar lists names still to draw: click one, then draw its outline</li>
          <li>A LiDAR suggestion's popup has <strong>Add as a listed boulder…</strong></li>
          <li>The workbench lists problems on the photo's boulder first, even unlocated ones, and drawing one gives it a location on that boulder. With no boulder outline, a problem without a location takes the photo's GPS instead (where the photo was taken), so it shows on the map ready to nudge into place</li>
        </ul>
        <p>On the public site, names label their outlines on the map when zoomed right in, and end the problem page's breadcrumb.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Photo inbox %>
    <section id="photo-inbox">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Photo Inbox (Upload Many Photos)" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Open the area and choose <strong>+ Add → Upload photos</strong>. Drop in as many photos as you like (JPEG, PNG or WebP, up to 25&nbsp;MB each). They upload one at a time; keep the tab open until they're done.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Each photo becomes a topo in the area straight away, so area admins can see it before any lines exist</li>
          <li>Photos with GPS are matched to the nearest boulder within 15&nbsp;m, shown as <strong>near boulder #N</strong>. That's only a hint for drawing</li>
          <li><strong>Photos to draw</strong> on the area's Topos page lists photos with no lines, or with a line still to draw</li>
          <li>Click a photo there (or <strong>Photo #N →</strong> after uploading) to draw its lines in the <a href="#topos" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">topo workbench</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="boulder-photos">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Boulder Photos from Visitors" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Visitors can send photos of boulders without choosing a problem, from <%= link_to "Add boulder photos", new_mapping_photo_contribution_path, class: "text-blue-600 hover:underline" %>. It's linked from the Contribute page, the Mapping page and each area's "Problems to map" list. After choosing an area they choose <strong>which boulder</strong> from a list of named boulders with no photo at all (the same rule as the admin Boulders list), or <strong>Another boulder, or not sure</strong>. Boulders without an outline are marked "Not on the map yet", and ones with a photo waiting for review are marked too.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Photos are checked in the browser first: JPEG/PNG/WebP under 15&nbsp;MB, and <strong>landscape only</strong> (topos are cropped to 4:3)</li>
          <li>Position comes from the photo's GPS. A photo without GPS taken in the last 15 minutes uses the phone's position instead, shown as <strong>(phone position)</strong></li>
          <li>A chosen boulder wins, and sets the area. Otherwise a located photo is matched to the nearest boulder within 15&nbsp;m in any published area. Cards say <strong>chosen</strong> or <strong>by GPS</strong>, and warn <strong>⚠ GPS puts it at …</strong> when the two disagree</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Reviewing:</strong> <%= link_to "Photos (N)", admin_photo_contributions_path, class: "text-blue-600 hover:underline" %> in the admin menu shows a grid of pending photos. Area admins see only their areas.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
          <li><strong>Accept</strong> makes the photo an undrawn topo in its area, just like a <a href="#photo-inbox" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Photo Inbox</a> upload, so it appears in <strong>Photos to draw</strong>. Accepted photos have a <strong>Draw lines →</strong> link</li>
          <li><strong>Decline</strong> keeps it in the closed list, with an optional note for the contributor</li>
          <li>Tick several, or <strong>Select all</strong>, to review them together</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Emails:</strong> staff get one email per upload session, not per photo. Contributors who gave an email get one acknowledgement per session and one email per review action.</p>
        <p><strong>Abuse and privacy:</strong> the invisible captcha checks the first photo of each session, and each visitor can send up to 120 photos an hour. Photos are only ever shown as resized copies with EXIF (GPS, camera) stripped, which now also applies to photos on public contribution pages. Accepted photos count towards the Top contributors leaderboard.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Photo Import %>
    <section id="photo-import">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Importing Problems from Photos" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>If you have a folder of photos named with problem names and grades, import them all with one command.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Filename convention</p>
        <pre class="bg-gray-800 text-gray-100 text-xs rounded-md p-4">Name_With_Underscores-grade.JPG

# Examples:
American_Squeeze_Job-7b+.JPG  →  "American Squeeze Job"  7b+
Walla_slab-5.JPG              →  "Walla slab"  5
The_Blimp-5+.JPG              →  "The Blimp"  5+</pre>

        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li>Use underscores for spaces in names</li>
          <li>Separate name and grade with a hyphen — the <strong>last</strong> hyphen is the separator</li>
          <li>Photos must be JPEGs with GPS coordinates in the EXIF (phone camera is fine)</li>
          <li>The folder name must match the area's slug or name (case-insensitive)</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Running the import</p>
        <pre class="bg-gray-800 text-gray-100 text-xs rounded-md p-4"># Folder inside the project root
rails import:problems FOLDER=bearacleave

# Absolute path
rails import:problems FOLDER=/path/to/haytor</pre>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">What gets created per photo</p>
        <table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 border border-gray-200 rounded-md overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-gray-50">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Record</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 uppercase">Values</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="bg-white divide-y divide-gray-100 text-xs">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">Problem</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Name, grade, steepness: wall, lat/lon from photo GPS EXIF</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">Topo</td><td class="px-4 py-2">The photo, attached via ActiveStorage</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono">Line</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Links topo to problem — no coordinates yet (draw manually in admin)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="bg-yellow-50 border border-yellow-200 rounded-md p-3 text-xs text-yellow-800">
          <strong>Note:</strong> Running the task twice on the same folder creates duplicates. Run once per folder. The area must already exist in admin before importing.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Location Editor %>
    <section id="location-editor">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Location Editor (Drag-and-Drop)" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Set GPS coordinates for unlocated problems directly in the browser — no GeoJSON export needed.</p>
        <p>Go to <strong>Admin → Areas → [area] → Map</strong> tab (or <strong>Map</strong> on the Areas list).</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">How to use</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
          <li>The <strong>left sidebar</strong> lists all problems without a location, sorted alphabetically</li>
          <li>The <strong>map</strong> shows boulder polygons for context and green pins for already-located problems</li>
          <li><strong>Drag</strong> a problem name from the sidebar and <strong>drop</strong> it on the map at the correct position — saved instantly</li>
          <li>To <strong>reposition</strong> an already-placed problem, drag its pin to a new spot — saves on release</li>
          <li><strong>Amber dashed</strong> pins are locations estimated from a photo. Drag each into its real spot and it turns into a normal pin (the legend counts how many are left)</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="bg-yellow-50 border border-yellow-200 rounded-md p-3 text-xs text-yellow-800">
          <strong>Bad coordinates?</strong> If problems were uploaded with wrong coordinates (e.g. all at the same point), clear them first:
          <pre class="mt-1 bg-yellow-100 rounded p-2 overflow-x-auto">bundle exec rails runner "Area.find_by(slug: 'area-slug').problems.update_all(location: nil)"</pre>
          Then use the location editor to place them correctly.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Adding Boulders %>
    <section id="adding-boulders">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Adding Boulders" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Boulders are grey polygon outlines shown on the map at zoom 16+. Each <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">Boulder</code> record stores a PostGIS polygon.</p>

        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-3">
          <li>
            <strong>Download the area's GeoJSON</strong> from the area edit page. This file contains existing problem points and boulder polygons.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Open in <a href="https://geojson.io" target="_blank" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">geojson.io</a></strong> — drag and drop the file. Switch to <strong>Satellite</strong> base layer.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Draw polygons</strong> around each boulder using the polygon tool (pentagon icon). No properties are needed — just the shape.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Export as GeoJSON</strong>, then upload via <strong>Admin → Imports → New</strong>. Preview the diff and click Apply.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-md p-3 text-xs text-blue-800">
          <strong>Tip:</strong> OpenStreetMap may already have boulder polygons for well-mapped areas. Export via
          <a href="https://overpass-turbo.eu" target="_blank" class="underline">Overpass Turbo</a>
          using the query <code>natural=rock</code>.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Boulder Editor %>
    <section id="boulder-editor">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Boulder Editor (In-Browser)" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Trace boulder outlines directly in the browser over satellite imagery. No GeoJSON export needed.</p>
        <p>Go to <strong>Admin → Areas → [area] → Outlines</strong> tab.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Drawing a new boulder</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Click <strong>Draw boulder</strong> — cursor changes to a crosshair</li>
          <li>Click on the map to place each vertex around the boulder outline</li>
          <li><strong>Double-click</strong> to finish and save — the polygon appears immediately</li>
          <li>Press <strong>Escape</strong> to cancel</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Editing an existing boulder</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Click any grey polygon on the map to select it (turns blue)</li>
          <li>Drag the blue vertex dots to reshape the outline</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Save changes</strong> to save, or click away to discard</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Deleting a boulder</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Click the polygon to select it</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Delete boulder</strong> and confirm</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">LiDAR hillshade overlay</p>
        <p>Under <strong>LiDAR hillshade</strong> in the sidebar, click <strong>LiDAR off</strong> to switch it on. Environment Agency 1&nbsp;m LiDAR is laid over the satellite image as shaded relief, so boulders show as crisp bumps with no shadows or lichen. Use the slider to blend it with the satellite image. Your choice is remembered in this browser.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>1&nbsp;m resolution: use it to find boulders and judge edges, and trace with the satellite visible underneath</li>
          <li>Satellite imagery can be a few metres out of place; where they disagree, trust the LiDAR</li>
          <li>Trees and gorse also show as bumps, but look softer and lumpier than rock</li>
          <li>Served live by the Environment Agency (England only). If it doesn't load, their service is down</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">LiDAR boulder suggestions</p>
        <p>Click <strong>Suggest boulders in this view</strong>. The editor analyses the Environment Agency's LiDAR heights for the current view and outlines anything that looks like rock: <strong>amber</strong> where nothing is mapped, <strong>green</strong> where it overlaps a mapped boulder (the LiDAR is often the more accurate outline).</p>
        <p>It finds both freestanding boulders and <strong>outcrops that rise out of the hillside</strong> (one vertical face, the back melting into the slope, as at Saddle Tor). For outcrops it looks for a vertical face and adds a 1.5&nbsp;m strip uphill for the top, so drag that uphill edge to where the rock really ends. A tor whose faces join up can come out as one big shape covering several boulders: trim it before using it. Earth banks and track cuttings can also look like faces.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Click a suggestion for its size and height, then <strong>Add as new boulder</strong> (saved and selected for tidying) or <strong>Replace outline of #N</strong> (not saved until you click <strong>Save changes</strong>)</li>
          <li>A selected boulder with an overlapping suggestion shows a <strong>Use LiDAR outline</strong> button</li>
          <li><strong>Add all N unmapped as boulders</strong> saves every amber suggestion at once (after a confirmation). Glance over them first for walls or cars</li>
          <li>Rock = over 1&nbsp;m above ground where the first and last laser returns agree, so gorse and trees are ignored; shapes under 3&nbsp;m² are left out</li>
          <li>Touching boulders often merge — you'll be warned when a suggestion covers several boulders; trim it before saving</li>
          <li>Outlines are good to about ±0.5&nbsp;m; boulders under 1&nbsp;m high are missed; walls and parked cars can appear</li>
          <li>Works on views under about 750&nbsp;m across. Click <strong>Suggest again</strong> after panning or editing</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-md p-3 text-xs text-blue-800">
          <strong>Tip:</strong> Zoom in to level 19 or higher before tracing — satellite detail is much clearer at high zoom. Click a boulder ID in the sidebar to fly the map to it. On a phone the sidebar sits above the map — scroll it to reach Basemap and LiDAR.
        </div>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Topo order on a boulder (automatic)</p>
        <p>Each boulder's published photos are linked to it and ordered anti-clockwise round the boulder, starting after the biggest gap between problems. This updates by itself within a few seconds when you change an outline, move a problem, draw or delete a line, or publish/unpublish a photo — there's no need to run <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">geo:compute</code> any more.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# GeoJSON Import %>
    <section id="geojson-import">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "GeoJSON Import Workflow" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Used to update problem locations and boulder polygons in bulk. Go to <strong>Admin → Imports → New</strong>.</p>

        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
          <li>Upload a <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">.geojson</code> file (must be a FeatureCollection)</li>
          <li>Preview page shows exactly what will be added or changed</li>
          <li>If there are conflicts (records edited since the file was exported) the import is blocked</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Apply</strong> to commit all changes</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">File rules</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li>All features must belong to the same area</li>
          <li><strong>Point</strong> features with a <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">problemId</code> property → update problem locations</li>
          <li><strong>Polygon</strong> features → update or create boulder outlines</li>
          <li>New polygons (no <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">boulderId</code>) create new boulder records</li>
        </ul>

        <%= link_to "Go to Imports →", admin_imports_path, class: "inline-block mt-2 text-blue-600 hover:underline text-sm" %>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Problem Editing %>
    <section id="problem-editing">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Individual Problem Editing" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Navigate to a problem via <strong>Admin → Areas → [area] → [circuit]</strong> then click a problem.</p>
        <p>The area's problem list is ordered by popularity (or circuit order) by default. Click the <strong>Id</strong>, <strong>Name</strong>, <strong>Grade</strong> or <strong>Steepness</strong> header to sort, and click again to reverse. Problems without a name or grade stay at the bottom.</p>
        <p>From the problem show page you can:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li>Edit name, grade, steepness, sit start, UKC URL</li>
          <li>Set or update location (lat/lon)</li>
          <li>Add a topo photo or draw lines on existing topos. Click a line's photo to open the line editor</li>
          <li>View and respond to contribution requests</li>
          <li>Link to a circuit via the edit form</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Problem Description %>
    <section id="problem-description">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Problem Description" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Each problem has an optional free-text description field for beta, key moves, starting instructions, or any other notes visible to users on the public problem page.</p>
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Adding or editing a description</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Open a problem in admin and click <strong>Edit</strong></li>
          <li>Fill in the <strong>Description</strong> field at the bottom of the form</li>
          <li>Save — the text will appear below the topo photo on the public problem page</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-xs">Leave the field blank and nothing will be shown. Plain text only — no markdown or HTML.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Topos %>
    <section id="topos">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Topos and Line Drawing" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Topos are photos with line drawings that show where to climb on a boulder face.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Uploading a topo</p>
        <p>Go to <strong>Admin → Topos → New</strong>. Upload the photo. Optionally upload a metadata JSON file (from the iOS app) to auto-link to problems.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Topo workbench (quickest)</p>
        <p>One page per photo to add every problem on it and draw all their lines without reloading. Open it from the area's <strong>Topos</strong> page (click a photo, or <strong>Photos to draw</strong>), straight after a photo upload, or from a topo or line page.</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Pick a problem on the right, or add one: filter the area's problems (ones <strong>on boulder</strong> first), or type a name and grade and <strong>Create and draw</strong>. New problems are placed on the matched boulder when the photo has GPS</li>
          <li>Click along the line from bottom to top, then press <strong>Enter</strong>. The next problem without a line is picked for you</li>
          <li>When they're all drawn, click <strong>Next photo to draw →</strong></li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>Boulder</strong> (top of the sidebar): the boulder the photo shows, tied by you, linked by its drawn problems, or matched by GPS. Pick another and click <strong>Tie</strong> (useful with no GPS, or for listed boulders not on the map yet); <strong>Not tied to a boulder</strong> goes back to the automatic match. New problems go on that boulder.</p>
        <p>A problem with no location gets an <strong>estimated</strong> one when it's added to a photo: from the photo's GPS (moved onto its boulder's outline), else from a problem already placed on the photo. Several from one photo, or any that would land on another problem, are spread about 2 m apart. Estimates show as amber pins in the location editor until you drag them into place.</p>
        <p>The yellow box lists that boulder's problems not on the photo yet: click one to add it, or <strong>Add all N</strong> to add them all and draw them in turn.</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">Keys: Enter save · Esc undo changes · Z undo point · N next undrawn problem · 1/2/3 line, start holds, not-in. On an already-drawn line the first click starts it afresh. ✕ removes a problem from the photo.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Drawing lines (one problem at a time)</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Open a problem's show page</li>
          <li>Click <strong>New line</strong> or edit an existing one</li>
          <li>Click on the photo to place control points</li>
          <li>Save — the line links the topo to the problem</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Marking start holds (optional)</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>In the line editor, switch <strong>Clicks add</strong> to <strong>Start holds</strong></li>
          <li>Click each starting hand hold (up to 4). Green rings mark them</li>
          <li>Save. They show as rings in the grade colour on the public problem page</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">To remove them, click <strong>Clear start holds</strong> and save. Holds are tied to that line's photo. Contributors can also mark them on the contribution form; they're copied over on accept.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Marking "not in" areas (optional)</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>In the line editor, switch <strong>Clicks add</strong> to <strong>Not-in areas</strong></li>
          <li>Click around the off-limits section (3+ points); click <strong>New not-in area</strong> to start another (up to 4)</li>
          <li>Save. They show as red hatched areas with a "Not in" key on the public problem page</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-500"><strong>Clear not-in areas</strong> removes them all. Contributors can mark them on the contribution form too.</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-500"><strong>Zoom:</strong> use <strong>Zoom − / +</strong> (1× or 2×) in the line editor toolbar to place holds and area corners precisely, and scroll to pan. The contribution form has the same buttons. Zoom only changes the view, not the saved points.</p>

        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Deleting a topo</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>Find the topo ID from the problem's show page</li>
          <li>Navigate to <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">/admin/topos/&lt;ID&gt;</code></li>
          <li>Click the red <strong>Delete topo</strong> button and confirm</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">Deleting a topo also permanently deletes all its associated lines.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Circuits %>
    <section id="circuits">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Circuits" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Circuits group problems by colour and difficulty. Each problem can belong to one circuit.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li>Circuit details (colour, risk) — super admin only</li>
          <li>Circuit membership is set per-problem via the problem edit form</li>
          <li>Circuits filter the map and appear on area pages</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# POIs %>
    <section id="pois">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "POIs and Routes" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Points of Interest (parking, train stations) help users navigate to areas.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li><strong>Admin → POIs</strong> — manage POI records (super admin only)</li>
          <li><strong>Admin → POI Routes</strong> — link POIs to areas with distance and transport type</li>
          <li>POI routes appear on the area edit page and in the mobile app</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Contributions %>
    <section id="contributions">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Contributions" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Users submit photos and route information via the public contribution form. The form includes:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li><strong>Boulder photo</strong> — GPS coordinates auto-extracted from photo EXIF where available</li>
          <li><strong>Route line</strong> — drawn on the uploaded photo using an in-browser canvas tool. Contributors can also select an existing topo from the area and draw on that instead of uploading a new photo.</li>
          <li><strong>Start holds</strong> — optional; the contributor switches the drawing tool to <strong>Start holds</strong> and clicks up to 4 starting hand holds (green rings)</li>
          <li><strong>Not in</strong> — optional; the contributor clicks around off-limits parts of the boulder (3+ points each, <strong>New area</strong> for another, up to 4)</li>
          <li><strong>UKC link</strong> — optional link to the problem on UK Climbing</li>
          <li><strong>GPS location</strong> — auto-populated from photo, or entered manually</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The location is set by dragging a pin on a map (it stays in sync with the lat/lon fields and the photo's EXIF GPS). The public form is rate-limited and honeypot-protected against spam, and rejects empty submissions.</p>
        <p>Go to <strong>Admin → Contributions</strong> to review submissions. The list opens on the <strong>pending</strong> queue, with photo thumbnails, dates and comment previews for quick triage, a pending-count badge, and pagination. The review page shows the boulder photo with any drawn route line, start holds and not-in areas overlaid, the UKC link, GPS coordinates, and contributor comments.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li><strong>pending</strong> — awaiting review</li>
          <li><strong>accepted</strong> — accepted and applied to the data</li>
          <li><strong>closed</strong> — declined</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-600">Contributors who left an email are notified automatically on submit, acceptance and decline. When declining, fill in the <strong>Note to contributor</strong> field to tell them why — it's included in the decline email. Staff alerts for new contributions go to the addresses in the <code>CONTRIBUTION_EMAILS</code> env var (comma-separated).</p>
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Partial acceptance</p>
        <p>When setting state to <strong>accepted</strong>, checkboxes control what gets applied. Their labels adapt to what the contribution contains:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li><strong>Line on Topo #N</strong> — the contributor drew on an existing topo; accepting only adds the line, no new photo is created</li>
          <li><strong>Photo &amp; line (new topo)</strong> — the contributor uploaded a photo and drew a line; accepting creates a new topo with the line overlay</li>
          <li><strong>Photo (new topo, no line drawn)</strong> — photo uploaded without a line</li>
          <li>Start holds and not-in areas go with the photo/line option (the label shows e.g. "+ 2 start holds and 1 not-in area") and are copied onto the new line. Ones marked on an existing topo without a line are imported as a line with just those</li>
          <li><strong>GPS coordinates</strong> — copies the contributor's location to the problem (only if the problem has no existing location; a hint shows if it's already located)</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-xs">Checkboxes are checked by default; if the contribution has no photo/line or no GPS, a "nothing to import" note shows instead. Uncheck either to skip that part — useful when the photo is good but the GPS is inaccurate, or vice versa. Accepting always closes any open contribution request for that problem.</p>
        <p>Accepting or closing returns you to the contributions list, with a banner summarising what was applied (e.g. "line added to Topo #74", with the topo linked) and how many contributions are still pending. Updates that don't change the state stay on the edit page.</p>
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Unlisted problems</p>
        <p>Submissions for a problem that isn't on the map yet arrive with a name/URL but no linked problem. The edit page shows a <strong>Create &amp; link problem</strong> form — pick the area and set name/grade/steepness to create a real problem (it inherits the submitted GPS), then accept to import the photo and line.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
          <li><strong>Area hint</strong> — a submission started from an area's mapping list carries that area (pre-selected; that area's admins can review it). Otherwise the contributor may type a crag name, shown on the edit page and marked <em>new?</em> in the list.</li>
          <li><strong>Nearest area</strong> — with GPS, the closest area within 1 km is pre-selected and its distance shown; with nothing nearby the page says it may be a new crag.</li>
          <li><strong>New crag</strong> (super admins) — fill in <strong>…or create a new area</strong> (pre-filled with the contributor's crag name when nothing is nearby). The area is created <strong>unpublished</strong> together with the problem; publish it from Areas once boulders are in.</li>
          <li><strong>Nearby submissions</strong> — other pending unlisted submissions within 300 m are listed; after the first creates the area, the rest suggest it.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Bulk close</p>
        <p>On the list, tick rows and use <strong>Close selected</strong> to decline several at once, with an optional shared note for the decline emails. The edit page also records when each contribution was accepted/closed and by whom.</p>
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Open data export</p>
        <p><strong>Currently development-only</strong> — disabled in production until the other contributors have agreed to the CC0 license. In development, the problems dataset is downloadable as CSV from <code>/data/problems.csv</code> (linked from the About page and footer). It includes <code>id, name, grade, steepness, latitude, longitude, area, url</code> for problems in <strong>published areas with a location</strong> — unpublished areas, unlocated problems and contributor details are never exported.</p>
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Boolder credit (footer)</p>
        <p>Every public page's footer ends with a credit line: "Built on Boolder, the open-source bouldering guide created for Fontainebleau. Thank you to the original Boolder team ♥". "Boolder" links to boolder.com; the thank-you links to the "Thank you, Boolder" section of the About page (<code>/about#thanks</code>). Wording lives in <code>config/locales/en.yml</code> under <code>views.layout.footer.boolder_credit_html</code> / <code>boolder_thanks</code>; markup in <code>app/views/layouts/_footer.html.erb</code>.</p>
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">Email delivery</p>
        <p>Contributor emails are skipped when the contribution has no email address (the field is optional on the public form). In <strong>development</strong> nothing is actually sent — emails are written to <code>tmp/mails/</code> for inspection. In <strong>production</strong>, sending uses the SMTP server configured under the <code>smtp</code> key in Rails credentials (address, port, username, password, from). See the "Email Setup" section of <code>docs/admin_guide.md</code> for the full checklist and how to test from the console.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Photo to the left / right %>
    <section id="topo-neighbours">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Photo to the Left / Right" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>On a public problem page, <strong>‹ ›</strong> buttons on the photo (and the <strong>← / →</strong> keys) step to the nearest photo to the left or right, as if you walked along the rocks. Each goes to the most climbed problem on that photo.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
          <li>A photo's position is the middle of its located problems (or where it was taken). Its facing direction is the inbox photo's compass heading, or is worked out from its boulder outline (you stand outside looking in).</li>
          <li>The neighbour is the nearest photo 20°–160° to that side, within 150 m, in the same area; photos from the same spot are skipped.</li>
          <li>No arrows show when the direction can't be worked out (no heading and no boulder outline). Locating the problems on a drawn boulder fixes that.</li>
          <li>The workbench sidebar and the admin topo page show each photo's arrows (click one to open that photo's workbench) or why it has none, including in unpublished areas.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Printable topo sheets %>
    <section id="topo-sheets">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Printable Topo Sheets" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Each published area has a <strong>Print topo sheet</strong> link under its problem list (<code>/dartmoor/&lt;area&gt;/print</code>): the area's warning and description, every published photo with its lines drawn and numbered, a numbered list under each photo, and a table of located problems without a photo yet.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Problems by grade %>
    <section id="problems-by-grade">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Problems by Grade and Map Grade Filters" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p><strong>Problems by grade</strong> (<%= link_to "/dartmoor/problems", problems_path, class: "text-blue-600 hover:underline" %>, linked from a card on the Dartmoor areas page) lists every published, located problem of one grade. Problems with a topo come first. The menu only offers grades that have problems.</p>
        <p>Sub-6a grades are entered simplified (4, 4+). Older detailed grades still match: 4 covers 4a–4b and 4+ covers 4b+–4c+. The map's grade filters use the same grouping.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Offline Use %>
    <section id="offline-use">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Offline Use (Map & Photos)" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p>Mobile signal often drops out on the moor, so every area page has a <strong>"Save map &amp; photos for offline"</strong> button (under the area description). One tap makes the whole area usable with no connection — both the topo photos and the interactive map.</p>
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-800">What gets saved</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-gray-600">
          <li><strong>Topo photos</strong> for the area</li>
          <li><strong>Problem pages</strong> for the area — tap a problem on the map offline and its page (topo + route line) opens</li>
          <li><strong>The base map</strong> for the area's bounding box — map tiles (zoom 0–14, over-zoomed for the close-up view), style, glyphs and sprites</li>
          <li><strong>Overlay data</strong> — the problem/boulder and area-label data shown on the map</li>
          <li><strong>The app shell</strong> — the area and map pages plus the JS/CSS (incl. the MapLibre map library) so the map renders with no network</li>
          <li><strong>The home page</strong> and area cover photos, so the app's start screen opens offline and lists the saved areas</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>My downloads</strong> (<a href="/downloads" class="text-granite-dark underline">/downloads</a>, footer link "Offline downloads"): every published area with Save / Remove, plus <strong>Save all areas</strong>. Saved areas show date and size; when an area has changed since it was saved (problems, boulders, topo lines/photos, cover) it shows <strong>Update available</strong> with an Update button (also flagged on the area page and the home page list). Removing an area keeps files other saved areas still need. With no connection — checked by pinging the server, since phones often show signal with nothing getting through — it lists just the saved areas. Code: <code>app/javascript/offline_areas.js</code>; versions from <code>Area.offline_versions</code>.</p>
        <p><strong>Searching offline:</strong> with no connection (or no answer within 5&nbsp;s) the search box searches the map data saved on the phone, offering only areas and problems whose pages are saved, under "Offline — showing results from your saved areas". Every word typed must appear in the name (case, accents and apostrophes ignored). Results now open the page directly rather than via <code>/redirects/new</code>. When the contents of a save change shape, bump <code>Area::OFFLINE_FORMAT</code> so saved copies show "Update available".</p>
        <p><strong>Reporting a mistake offline:</strong> every problem page links <strong>"Report a mistake"</strong>, which opens the report form set to that problem — reports arrive in Contributions linked to it. With no signal the form still opens (it's saved with each area) and the report is kept on the phone, then sent automatically the next time any page opens with a connection, with a confirmation message. Sending takes ~5&nbsp;s because of the spam check's 4-second minimum. Code: <code>offline_reports.js</code>, <code>report_form_controller.js</code>.</p>
        <p><strong>Installing the app:</strong> the site installs as an app (PWA), which keeps saved areas safe — Safari deletes a website's saved data after about 7 days without a visit, but not a home-screen app's. On Android/desktop Chrome an <strong>"Install the app"</strong> button appears on the home page and under the save button; on iPhone/iPad the same spots say <em>tap Share, then Add to Home Screen</em>. The installed app opens on the home page, whose <strong>"Saved for offline"</strong> list leads to the saved areas; with no signal, unsaved pages show a "You're offline" page listing them too.</p>
        <p>A single area is only a few MB. After saving, a timestamp and a <strong>Remove</strong> link appear; Remove clears that area's tiles, photos and pages. If some files fail to download, the saved message says how many — re-save on a better connection.</p>
        <p><strong>Checking a saved area:</strong> the <a href="/offline-status" class="text-granite-dark underline">offline status page</a> ("Details" next to a saved area) shows the service worker version, cache contents, the pinned tile snapshot, and whether each saved area is complete. If offline mode misbehaves in the field, a screenshot of that page is the bug report.</p>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-xs">Implementation: a service worker (<code>public/service-worker.js</code>) serves cached assets offline — it also handles Turbo Drive link clicks (not just full navigations) and races the network against a 3.5&nbsp;s timeout so a weak signal falls back to cache quickly. <code>offline_download_controller.js</code> pre-fetches everything using the area's bounds/URLs from <code>Areas::OfflineDataController</code>, counting failures, and re-pins a <em>fresh</em> OpenFreeMap style + TileJSON on every save (their tile URLs contain a rotating dated snapshot — downloading against a stale one yields an empty map). End-to-end check: <code>script/offline_check.mjs</code>. <strong>Known limitation:</strong> problems in a <em>different</em>, un-downloaded area still need a connection.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Stats %>
    <section id="stats">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Stats" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p><strong>Admin → Stats</strong> (super admins only): page views for the chosen period (7–365 days) with a daily chart and the most viewed areas and problems; weekly activity over 12 weeks (problems, lines, photos, contributions, visitor photos, admin edits); contributions waiting and the typical wait for a decision; mapping progress per area; and who edited what in the last 30 days.</p>
        <p><strong>How visits are counted:</strong> anonymous daily totals per page (home, area, problem, map) in <code>page_views</code>, with no IP address, cookie, browser details or visitor ID, as the privacy policy says. Only real visits count (a page opened in the browser or by following a link); hover prefetches, offline downloads, admins, bots and error pages don't. Counting started on 4 November 2026.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Search engines %>
    <section id="search-engines">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Search engines" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p><code>/sitemap.xml</code> lists every public page (published areas, their shown and located problems, circuits) with the date each last changed. It's built from the database when requested and cached for an hour, so new problems appear straight away and hidden ones never do. <code>robots.txt</code> points crawlers at it. Problem, area and map pages also name their preferred (canonical) address.</p>
        <p>To see what search engines have indexed, add the site to Google Search Console and Bing Webmaster Tools and submit <code>https://bowda.rocks/sitemap.xml</code> there.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Support page %>
    <section id="support-page">
      <%= render "admin/docs/section_header", title: "Support Page (Donations)" %>
      <div class="prose-content text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-4">
        <p><code>/support</code> ("Support Bowda" in the footer) asks visitors to chip in towards the running costs, with a target of £75 a year, and explains in plain terms where that goes: server, the bowda.rocks address, spare copies and headroom.</p>
        <p>The "Buy Bowda a coffee" button goes to the <code>SUPPORT_URL</code> env var, or the <code>support_url</code> credential, e.g. a Ko-fi page. With neither set, the button is hidden and the page says donations open soon. It's a plain link, so no third-party scripts load. To change the figures, edit the <code>costs</code> list at the top of <code>app/views/pages/support.html.erb</code>, along with the "£75" wording in its intro and heading.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</div>
